meta {
  name: Get Options
  type: http
  seq: 4
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/analytics/options
  body: none
  auth: bearer
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{analyticsToken}}
}

tests {
  test("returns 200 OK", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });

  test("lists branches, producers, assignees and carriers", function () {
    const body = res.getBody();
    expect(body.branches.length).to.be.at.least(1);
    expect(body.producers.length).to.be.at.least(1);
    expect(body).to.have.property("assignees");
    expect(body.carriers).to.be.an("array");
    // Alias codes are folded into their carrier.
    expect(body.carriers).to.not.include("B4tEH");
  });
}

docs {
  # GET /analytics/options — what the filter bar can offer

  - **Module:** `analytics`
  - **Permission:** `analytics:read` (Agency Owner via enabled modules, Branch Manager by template; grantable to anyone)
  - **Data scope:** sales use the dashboards' clamp — agency sees the agency, branch sees their branch, own sees their own sales. `branchId` narrows an **agency-scope** caller to one office; a branch- or own-scope caller may name only their own branch, and `BranchGuard` refuses any other with a `403`.

  Served behind `analytics:read` alone, so a producer granted the page can
  filter without `agency:users:read`. Branches and producers are the Data
  Export page's list (same scope rules). `assignees` are the people tickets in
  the caller's ticket scope are assigned to — the Service tab's person filter.
  `carriers` are the agency's policy carriers, normalised and deduplicated.

  ## Response `200`

  ```json
  {
    "branches": [{ "id": "…", "name": "Main" }],
    "producers": [{ "id": "…", "name": "Pat Producer" }],
    "assignees": [{ "id": "…", "name": "Casey Kim" }],
    "carriers": ["Allstate", "Progressive"]
  }
  ```
}
